# Canonical identities.
#
# Git reads this for log, shortlog and blame, and GitHub reads it for contributor
# attribution, so an author who committed under an earlier name shows up correctly
# everywhere without a single commit being rewritten.
#
# The alternative here would have been rewriting the first twenty-one commits. That was
# rejected for a reason worth recording: every directory under benchmarks/results/ is named
# after the commit that produced it, and each environment.json records that hash. Changing
# the hashes would leave every published measurement pointing at a commit that no longer
# exists, and the chain from a number back to the code that produced it is the thing this
# project is built on.

Madhav Kothandaraman <info@gravaxis.in> Ishwar <info@gravaxis.in>
